+
+## The research this library owes, because it is security-sensitive
+
+**`nsockets` is security-sensitive** (@@PD-168@@): it parses addresses from text (`ADDRESS_MODEL.md` A-19 … A-24, and
+`SK-036` treats its refusals as a security claim), walks control messages another process sent (`ANCILLARY_MODEL.md`
+N-9 … N-11, and `VERIFICATION.md` Z-3: "the only place in this library where attacker-adjacent binary data drives an
+offset"), passes descriptors between processes (N-2) and reads a peer's credentials (N-13 … N-17). So the workbench
+research skill's §8 applies, and its three requests are owed, each in its §3 shape and `sensitivity: security` — two
+independent primaries, stale after ninety days — each filed here as a digest its row cites. Each request, with what
+would change the plan if it came true, is written out in full in cycle 0.0.0's plan, §13:
+
+1. **Known defects** in the prior art and the reference implementations of the five surfaces the library parses or
+   decodes: IPv4 and IPv6 text addresses, `sockaddr_un` paths, `cmsghdr` walks, `SCM_RIGHTS`, and
+   `SO_PEERCRED`/`SCM_CREDENTIALS`. *Would change the plan if* a defect class reaches one of the five that no rule
+   refuses or tests.
+2. **The standards bodies' current guidance** for the surfaces the rules rest on: the IPv6 addressing architecture
+   and text form (RFC 4291, RFC 5952), IPv4 dotted-quad strictness, IPv4-mapped addresses, and POSIX.1-2024's socket
+   interfaces. *Would change the plan if* a cited RFC is obsoleted or updated where a rule depends on it, current
+   guidance contradicts a rule, or POSIX specifies an interface the library uses differently from the Linux
+   behaviour its specifications state.
+3. **The current test vectors** for IPv4 and IPv6 text-address parsing and formatting, and for `cmsghdr` and
+   `SCM_RIGHTS` handling, with their versions and primary copies. *Would change the plan if* a maintained set exists
+   that cycle 0.1's conformance stage should pin, or a set the specifications cite has a newer revision.
+
+**They gated cycle 0.0's work** (@@PD-168@@): cycle 0.0.0's research gate filed the three digests here on 2026-10-29,
+before any probe ran, and the plan was re-cut that day on the libraries' seat's rulings. The orchestrator dispatches the
+requests to the researcher, who never writes into a repository. A finding that asked the language a question became a
+cycle-0.0 probe; every other is held where its subject is decided — a row of this table, a specification, or the
+roadmap item of the cycle that owns it — or this section says who decides it; none is dropped for want of a probe (the
+workbench plan skill's note of 2026-10-16). What each digest found, and where each finding is held:
+
+- **[`security-defects.md`](security-defects.md)**, item 1, found published defects in all five surfaces. An IPv4
+  octet's leading zero read as octal (CVE-2021-29921, -29922 and -29923) is held by `ADDRESS_MODEL.md` A-19 and
+  `SK-036`'s dated note; `::` and group counting by A-20 and A-22, with `TESTING.md` V-9's fixed cases; an overlong
+  `sun_path` by A-13. Initialisation on the IPv4-in-IPv6 path asked the language, and probe 15 answers its direct read:
+  refused, `NITPICK-ASSIGN-001` (A-22's dated note). The `cmsghdr` walk's arithmetic, its final empty header and its
+  tail padding, which no rule held, are `ANCILLARY_MODEL.md` N-19, with V-12's fixed cases, and the `uint64`
+  subtraction N-19 relies on traps as `IntOverflow` (cycle 0.0.0's §1.4, the gate's P1'). The credential defects are
+  held by N-13 … N-17.
+- **[`security-guidance.md`](security-guidance.md)**, item 2, found RFC 4291 current and RFC 5952 its text form's
+  update. A-19's leading-zero refusal stands, deliberately stricter than POSIX.1-2024's `inet_pton` (Austin Group
+  Defect 1573): its sentence names glibc and RFC 3986 §3.2.2, its table gains the two spellings where they part, and
+  `SK-036` its dated note. A-20 already accepts every RFC 4291 form, and reads its dotted-quad tail by A-19's rules;
+  `::a.b.c.d` parses and is never converted (`SK-037`). RFC 5952's own status page did not answer, so its row above
+  carries a second read. POSIX's one documented difference from Linux, `accept`'s `O_NONBLOCK` inheritance, goes to
+  `COMPAT.md`, whose matrix cycle 0.10 fills.
+- **[`security-vectors.md`](security-vectors.md)**, item 3, found no maintained vector set for the whole semantics.
+  Two cross-check corpora are rows above, os-test's `inet_ntop.c` and glibc's `tst-inet_pton.c`; the `cmsghdr` and
+  `SCM_RIGHTS` sets are test programs only, read by cycle 0.5, which writes the walk.
+
+A later cycle reads the three dates before it starts, since each is stale ninety days after its own, and all three are
+re-run at hardening, cycle 0.10.
